  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 AM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nA 1002mb low swirls over far southwest WI early this morning \nwhile a lagging cold front bulges into western IL. An area of \nrain is lifting through the Ohio River Valley beneath 75-knot \n500mb flow and a 50-knot 850-mb jet. Rain today will generally \namount to 0.10- 0.25\". Far northwest OH might see slightly \nmore based on the trajectory of the heaviest returns upstream, \nin an area of favorable moisture convergence aided by the \naformentioned jets. Forecast soundings show little chance of \nany deep low-level mixing today which will keep wind gusts in \ncheck; primarily 20 mph to 30 mph. Rain tapers off from west to \neast by early afternoon as the dryslot moves overhead. Cold air \nadvection is delayed until late in the day due to boundary- \nparallel flow and will be most noticeable by Thursday when with \nlows in the mid- 40s and highs shy of 60 degrees (a touch mild \nstill for early November). \n\nAfter a dry start to Thursday, a secondary trough axis ripples \nthrough in the late afternoon providing ample lift for scattered \nshowers. Reinforcing cold air (2C at 850mb) over a 10-13C Lake \nMichigan results in lake enhanced showers that persist into \nSaturday morning. (If the airmass were colder, this would be a\nsolid LES setup.) Friday and Saturday are the coldest of this \nforecast period as deep troughing is entrenched over the eastern\nGreat Lakes. \n\nTemperatures inch upward early next week as we lie on the cusp of a \nsouth-central US ridge. How far east this ridge propagates is \nuncertain  with the prospect of a potential blocking pattern taking \nshape over the interior Rockies. For now, precipitation chances \nappear minimal, but a shift in energetic northwest flow could flip \nthe script if the ridge is not as strong, bringing passing chances \nfor light rain.",
